# Copy this file to `.env` before running anything:
#
#     cp .env.example .env        (macOS/Linux)
#     copy .env.example .env      (Windows)
#
# `.env` itself is gitignored and never committed — it is per-machine.
# This file exists so a fresh clone knows what to put in it.

# The local development database. SQLite, a single file in this folder,
# initialized by `node scripts/init-local-db.mjs`, then populated by
# `npx prisma migrate deploy`. Nothing else to install.
#
# Postgres on managed hosting is the decision for production (DEC-32), but
# that host has not been chosen yet — IN-02. Until then this is the only
# database anybody runs.
DATABASE_URL="file:./dev.db"

# Where uploaded files go. Local disk is the only driver today and is right
# for a laptop; it does not survive most deployments. See src/lib/storage.ts
# and IN-01.
# STORAGE_DRIVER="local"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sharing the site with somebody who is not at this machine.
#
# The site is not deployed (IN-01), so the only way to show it to somebody
# remote is a Cloudflare quick tunnel, which puts it on a public
# *.trycloudflare.com hostname. Public means public: every seeded account
# shares the password `123456` (SEC-01), and behind the login are supplier cost
# prices, margins and customer accounts.
#
# So the tunnel is never opened without these. PREVIEW_PASSWORD puts HTTP Basic
# in front of every request — pages, assets, API routes — before the
# application's own sign-in is reached. Leave it unset and there is no gate at
# all, which is correct for local development.
#
# Generate a fresh one per share, and send it to the person separately from the
# link. See src/lib/preview-gate.ts.
# PREVIEW_USER="aussiemed"
# PREVIEW_PASSWORD=""

# The address links in emails should point at. Set it to the tunnel URL while
# sharing, so a link in the outbox is clickable for the person looking.
# PUBLIC_URL="https://something.trycloudflare.com"
